-Even in problems with exact ties, CEU needs a choice that the normality framework does not settle. Without statism, BRN distinguish belief conditional on $q$ (their definition restricts accessibility to $q$ while keeping the current evidence's normality relations) from belief after discovering $q$. They show that the first can hold without the second (App. D.3). Their example is a variant of Flipping for Heads from Goodman and Salow (2023, 138). The coin is fair; $q$ says it was flipped either once or more than $n$ times. Normality comes from probability by BRN's threshold rule. I take $n=4$ and $t=0.9$, so that one initially believes, and in the actual state $s_1$ (heads first) knows, that there were at most four flips. On the hypothesis $q$ one believes there was one flip. After discovering $q$ one leaves five flips open.
-
-Offer twenty-to-one on "at most four flips" and the free question whether $q$. KDT values the question at $-0.53$. Looking changes the payoff exactly on $q$, so $D=q$. On BRN's reading of conditional knowledge, the ken on $D$ at $s_1$ is $\{s_1\}$, and CEU prefers betting now. On the discovery reading it is $\{s_1,s_5\}$, and CEU prefers looking. The same split arises for Salow's qualitative principle restricted to accuracy about the bet's proposition. With BRN's conditional belief, you believe that keeping your belief is better on the hypothesis that the question makes a difference, so Conditional Knowledge Dominance says not to look. In this example the repair works on the post-discovery reading and fails on the specified alternative reading. That is Salow's constraint, and outside statist models BRN's account of conditional belief rejects it. Their reason is not idle: conditional belief must make sense for hypotheses one could never learn, such as "p, but I will never learn that p" (App. D.3). A defender of the repair can keep both only by letting the practical role of conditional knowledge diverge from its doxastic role exactly in the non-statist cases that BRN take to be typical.
