 ### 6.2 "Trivial once stated: knowledge is treated as certainty"
 
-The pieces are indeed familiar. Lemma 1 is an ordinary bet, Lemma 2 is Geanakoplos's sufficiency condition, and Corollary (i) parallels BRN's result. Three things are new. The first is the exact location of the boundary, which includes abnormal situations whose kens extend below the belief set. The second is Theorem 2's completion-independence: no belief-containing state other than a gapped one can be conditioned on, so no refinement of knowledge (iterated knowledge, justified true belief) escapes. The third is Theorem 3. Harman's and Kripke's discussions explain why knowledge-as-certainty invites the puzzle. They do not show that the leading conditional remedy fails in expected-utility form. Nor do they show that, on graded normality, conditioning on evidence is the only way out.
+The pieces are indeed familiar. Lemma 1 is an ordinary bet, Lemma 2 is Geanakoplos's sufficiency condition, and Corollary (i) parallels BRN's result. The proposed contribution has three parts. The first is the exact location of the boundary, which includes abnormal situations whose kens extend below the belief set. The second is Theorem 2's completion-independence: no belief-containing state other than a gapped one can be conditioned on, so no refinement of knowledge (iterated knowledge, justified true belief) escapes. The third is Theorem 3. Harman's and Kripke's discussions explain why knowledge-as-certainty invites the puzzle. They do not show that the leading conditional remedy fails in expected-utility form. The necessity result concerns fixed conditioning states and the stated universal safety requirement, not every response to dogmatism.
 
 ### 6.3 "KDT is meant for low stakes"
 
-Stakes-sensitive variants condition on higher-order knowledge when stakes rise (Goldschmidt 2024 surveys them). Under K-normality with transparency, knowledge iterates, so such variants coincide with KDT here. The stakes needed are moderate in any case. Bjorn's case needs only ten-to-one, where the fallibilist bets too.
+Under K-normality with transparency, iterating knowledge does not change the ken, so conditioning on higher-order knowledge gives the same probabilities here. A ten-to-one payoff ratio is not an absolute measure of stakes. At a fixed ken, positive rescaling of all utilities preserves choices and the sign of information value, so the counterexample can have arbitrarily small payoff magnitudes. But if the ken itself changes with the menu or practical stakes, the theorem must be applied to that changed model. It does not refute every stakes-sensitive theory.
 
 ### 6.4 "Statism is the exception"
 
-BRN argue that statism fails in general. Three replies. Bjorn's model, which carries §3, is statist. Lemma 4 survives without statism in BRN's simple probability structures. If $m_0$ is the most probable member of $p_v$, then $p_v$ consists of the states no more probable than $m_0$. "Not $m_0\gg v$" says that the probability, given $E\cap p_v$, of the states more probable than $v$ is below threshold. By BRN's characterization of belief (Prop. 5) this puts $v$ in $B(E\cap p_v)$. And non-statist models only add failures, as §5.3 shows: discovering what one already knows can destroy knowledge (Goodman and Salow 2023, §8).
+BRN argue that statism fails in general. The finite equivalence is conditional on statism, and it should not be advertised as a classification of their entire framework. Bjorn's measurement model is statist, however, so that restriction leaves a substantive application. Beyond it, the relevant questions are whether the post-answer kens remain nested and whether the questions used in Lemma 4 still reopen the excluded possibilities. Neither condition should be assumed without proof.
 
 ### 6.5 Encroachment
 
-Salow (2025, 24 n. 3) reports Hawthorne's (2004, 181) suggestion that, given Good's theorem, the puzzle is handled by pragmatic encroachment of the kind on which encroachment occurs whenever the difference between maximal and actual probability affects one's preferences over the available options. Salow objects that encroachment would not be confined to failures of Preservation (36). Theorem 2 makes the cost exact. To guarantee safety for every decision problem and every question in the theorem's class on graded evidence, a factive belief-containing state must shrink to the evidence. This does not show that the mere availability of a second scale destroys all inductive knowledge at every fixed stake level. The universal guarantee quantifies over decision problems as well as questions.
+Salow (2025, 24 n. 3) reports Hawthorne's (2004, 181) suggestion that, given Good's theorem, the puzzle is handled by pragmatic encroachment of the kind on which encroachment occurs whenever the difference between maximal and actual probability affects one's preferences over the available options. Salow objects that encroachment would not be confined to failures of Preservation (36). Theorem 2 makes the cost exact. To guarantee safety for every decision problem and every question in the theorem's class on graded evidence, a factive belief-containing state must expand to include the entire evidence set. This does not show that the mere availability of a second scale destroys all inductive knowledge at every fixed stake level. The universal guarantee quantifies over decision problems as well as questions.
 
 ### 6.6 "Good's theorem is not sacrosanct"
 
-Ahmed and Salow (2019) argue that Good's theorem may fail. What is rational, on their view, is to make one's act depend on the answer to a question, and looking first is rational exactly when it is a way of doing that. Their counterexamples involve inexact, non-partitional observation. Bjorn's second weighing is not of that kind. The reading is observed exactly, the evidence is transparent, and the possible readings partition the states. Looking is therefore a way of making his act depend on the answer to a question, which is the advice they endorse. So the case does not belong among their counterexamples. The challenge is therefore to explain why avoidance in this partitional measurement case is rational, rather than assimilating it without further argument to their non-partitional examples.
-
-### 6.7 Carter and Hawthorne's inquiry-based dogmatism
-
-Carter and Hawthorne (2024) consider Ex Ante Dogmatism, the recommendation that one avoid gathering evidence bearing on what one knows. They ask how a view on which inquiry aims at knowledge can resist it. The present result is about expected utility, not the aims of inquiry. But it locates exactly where a knowledge-conditioned decision rule recommends ex ante avoidance (non-gapped kens), and, for the finite theorem's question class, it shows that every proper factive belief-containing ken in graded evidence has some unsafe question.
+Ahmed and Salow (2019) contest unrestricted requirements to gather and use information. Bjorn's readings partition the evidential possibilities exactly, but the knowledge kens used for decisions can still overlap. Thus exact observation does not by itself put the decision problem back inside the classical partitional theorem. The negative result is a cost for a theorist who wants universal safety, not a contradiction for one who rejects it.
+
+Another response allows the agent to commit to ignoring an answer. If that commitment is feasible, the menu of policies includes the present best act, so the best available policy cannot be worse merely because a free signal is offered. The results concern looking followed by the specified future choice rule, without such commitment. Their practical significance is the resulting tension between present evaluation and later rational choice. Whether knowledge should determine numerical probabilities, whether the later rule should change, or whether such tensions are acceptable remains a philosophical choice.
 
 ## 7. Conclusion
 
-In finite statist, comparable models with K-normality and the specified questions available, expected utility conditioned on knowledge makes every free question safe exactly where knowledge is gapped. Any factive state that leaves open what rational belief leaves open needs the same condition. On graded normality, which Goodman and Salow defend for measurement, that condition means not going beyond the evidence. The expected-utility form of Salow's Conditional Knowledge Dominance does not escape: it disvalues free evidence wherever the unconditional rule does, and in non-statist models its success depends on identifying conditional with post-discovery knowledge. What has not been shown is that no other way of letting all-out attitudes guide graded choice could preserve Salow's dissolution. The result places a precise burden on this conditionalizing form of knowledge-first decision theory. It must weaken the universal safety requirement, restrict its model or question class, or change how knowledge controls numerical choice. Collapse is one sufficient restriction, and evidence-based probabilities are one alternative; the results do not establish an exhaustive trilemma for every knowledge-first theory.
